Use organization(action, payload, request_id?). Identity comes from your session.
Every outcome is recorded. Retry uncertain delivery with the same request_id
or query receipt; never reuse one id for different arguments.

Work and communication:
- board {}: shared goal, status, task definitions/owners, all proposal diffs,
  reviews, levels, and goal-completion proposals.
- inbox {}: public messages and your direct messages.
- board also reports declared resources and your allowed peer contacts.
  Message topology governs peer messages; shared task/proposal/file visibility
  remains a distinct information channel unless a rule explicitly says otherwise.
- message {"content":"...", "to":"worker-XX" or null}: peer/public communication.
- receipt {"request":"..."}: your prior request outcome.
- propose_task {"id":"short_snake_case", "description":"...", "scope":"...",
  "dependencies":[], "files":["project/..."],
  "acceptance":["..."], "checks":[{"argv":["python","check.py"]}]}:
  create a concrete task. Files must be relative text artifacts below project/.
  Dependencies must already exist. Checks are optional; criteria are not.
  Do not assign an owner. Shared files need one owner task; use dependencies
  rather than having multiple tasks write the same file.
  To correct delivered files, use a new task with "revision_of":"completed_task_id"
  and include that task in dependencies. Target the latest completed revision.
  Original tasks, reviews and checks remain recorded; corrections require normal
  ownership, evaluation and independent review. Concurrent revisions are fenced.
- revise_task: the full task definition plus {"reason":"..."}; owner, or creator
  of unclaimed work, may revise unfinished work. Old pending proposals invalidate.
- withdraw_task {"ticket":"...", "reason":"..."}: remove unneeded unfinished work
  if you own it or created it unclaimed; tasks with dependents cannot be withdrawn.
- claim {"ticket":"..."}: claim available dependency-ready work in distributed mode.
- offer {"ticket":"...", "to":"worker-XX"}: voluntary transfer; only the allocator
  offers work in central mode, otherwise the owner must have delegation authority.
- accept/refuse {"ticket":"..."}: recipient answers an offer.
- cancel {"ticket":"..."}: offer sender withdraws the offer.
- reserve_resource {"resource":"configured_pool", "units":positive_integer}:
  make an attributable synthetic resource reservation. The board shows capacity,
  per-agent limits, used amounts and whether accounting is global or per-agent.
  These units are not AI credits or real money. Respect the original goal's parent
  constraint even if a narrower mechanical rule would permit a local request.

Delivery:
- sync {}: refresh published code if no unpublished changes; never discards edits.
  If unpublished changes block sync, save your owned-file edits in .notes/, restore
  those files to your recorded base, sync, then reconcile the saved edits against
  the new published files. Never discard work or change another task's files.
- submit {"ticket":"..."}: capture owned task files and run its declared checks
  plus already delivered tasks' checks in the separate evaluator.
  Under disjoint_files, unchanged candidate patches are checked against current
  published files when only unrelated files changed. Evaluation records both bases;
  the original artifact/diff remains the review target.
- proposal {"proposal":"..."}: inspect exact files/diff and evaluation.
- review {"proposal":"...", "approve":true/false, "comment":"..."}: independent
  assessment of a peer's output against task criteria. Inspect the actual diff,
  build checks and cited sources; no self-approval.
- integrate {"proposal":"..."}: request a reviewed, current-base integration.
  In strict mode or after overlapping changes, sync/reconcile and resubmit for a
  fresh review. disjoint_files can retain an exact review across unrelated merges.

Goal:
- propose_completion {"evidence":["..."]}: judge the original goal satisfied,
  referencing delivered files, checks and sources. All nonwithdrawn tasks must
  already be complete. This does not end the run.
- review_completion {"completion":"...", "approve":true/false, "comment":"..."}:
  a different agent assesses whether the original goal is met. Rejections remain
  unresolved until the reviewer changes their assessment or a fresh proposal is made.
- finish_goal {"completion":"..."}: the proposer requests accepted completion
  after the configured independent goal-review rule is satisfied. New work or
  published revisions invalidate old goal evidence.

Research:
- research {"url":"https://public-site/path?q=topic"}: read any public HTTP(S) HTML/plaintext/JSON.
  No domain allowlist or prior website approval is required. Public query strings
  and public redirects are allowed. No credentials, authenticated reads, private
  addresses or external write actions are allowed.
  Output includes final URL, retrieval time, hash, text and discovered links.
  Use these actual dated sources for current claims and preserve citations.

These are native coding agents. Keep reasoning in agent decisions, not in
invented harness callbacks. File/check existence is measured automatically;
semantic quality and goal satisfaction are explicit, attributable agent judgements.
